My advice: **the formula is easy, the valid metric is hard.** I would not use per-keystroke productivity as the basis for individual performance evaluation or compensation. The measure is easily gamed: developers can write verbose code, split changes, avoid deletions, or use macros. Nor do languages, editors, IDEs, and codebases have the same "keystrokes per semantic unit."

A more defensible formula assigns weight to the **change**, not to each keystroke individually:

$$
\text{PK}_d=\frac{\sum_{c\in\text{commits}_d}\big(\alpha v(c)+\beta f(c)+\gamma r(c)-\lambda w(c)-\mu b(c)\big)}{|\text{keystrokes}_d|}
$$

where:

- $v(c)$: semantically valuable additions, such as new functionality that is accepted and tested;
- $f(c)$: bug fixes, weighted by severity;
- $r(c)$: refactoring that reduces complexity or technical debt;
- $w(c)$: rework, such as reverts and changes made within a short time because of errors;
- $b(c)$: defects and incidents attributable to the change;
- $\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\lambda,\mu$: weights calibrated from your own data.

You can refine $v(c),f(c),r(c)$ from diffs, static analysis, test coverage, code reviews, and later incidents. But **one keystroke has no independent value**: the same character can be noise in one place and a fix in another.

If you want a simpler metric, use this instead:

$$
\text{Productivity}_d=\frac{\text{Semantic Value}_d-\text{Rework Cost}_d}{\text{Time}_d}
$$

This measures not how many characters were typed, but **what was delivered, how much had to be redone, and how long it took**. As a first step, use the index to diagnose tooling friction, not to rank developers.

